<div class="hero">
  <div class="hero-overline">In the browser, free</div>
  <h1 class="hero-title">SVG Map Editor</h1>
  <p class="hero-lede">Take an SVG map and put it in your own colors — recolor by fill and stroke group, hide the layers you do not need, undo anything, then export. The file is parsed in your browser and never uploaded anywhere.</p>
</div>

<div class="btn-row" style="margin-top: var(--space-4)">
  <a href="/tools/svg-editor/app/" class="btn btn--filled">Open the editor</a>
</div>

<hr class="rule rule--heavy">

<section class="section">
  <div class="section-label">What you can do</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">Three things, well</h2>

  <div class="prose content-column">
    <p><b>Recolor by group.</b> The editor reads the SVG's fill and stroke values and groups every shape that shares one, so changing a color changes every shape using it at once — not one path at a time.</p>
    <p><b>Turn layers off.</b> Most maps carry more than you need. Per-layer visibility lets you strip a map back to the parts that matter before you restyle what is left.</p>
    <p><b>Export what you made.</b> Out comes an SVG you can drop into a document, a slide, or a print file, at whatever size you want, because it is still vector.</p>
  </div>
</section>

